  "analysis": [
    {
      "label": "Context",
      "text": "Argentina were 1–0 up after Maxi Rodríguez's early goal and had no need to force anything. The move that followed is remembered for its roughly 24 passes before Cambiasso's finish."
    },
    {
      "label": "Circulation",
      "text": "Switching play from Sorín on the left to Maxi on the right made Serbia and Montenegro's block shuffle across again and again. Each shift pulled a midfielder a step out of line, and gaps started to open between the units."
    },
    {
      "label": "Why it worked",
      "text": "Players like Mascherano and Riquelme kept offering short angles, so a pass was always on and pressing became pointless. Chasing the ball tired the defenders and stretched their shape instead of winning it back."
    },
    {
      "label": "The finish",
      "text": "Once the tempo rose, Saviola and Crespo dragged defenders towards the ball in the box. Crespo's backheel reversed the direction of play, and Cambiasso arrived unmarked to hit it first time."
    }
  ]
